  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In modern terms oxidation is loss of electrons; the oxygen and hydrogen definitions are older special cases.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zinc loses two electrons to become Zn²⁺, so it is oxidised and acts as the reducing agent.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It takes electrons from the other species and is itself reduced.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2(+1) + x + 4(−2) = 0, so x = +6.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2(+1) + 2x + 7(−2) = 0, so 2x = 12 and x = +6.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1 + x + 4(−2) = 0, so x = +7.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Atoms of the same element in a pure element share electrons equally, so the oxidation number is zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    x + 4(−2) = −2, so x = +6.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Chlorine's oxidation number falls from 0 to −1, so it is reduced and acts as the oxidising agent.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zinc's oxidation number rises from 0 to +2 while hydrogen's falls from +1 to 0.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Oxidation is loss of electrons, which happens where electrons are removed — the positive electrode, the anode.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sodium ions gain electrons at the cathode and are reduced to sodium metal.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Chloride ions are discharged in preference to hydroxide ions at the anode, giving chlorine.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sodium ions remain in solution with the hydroxide ions produced at the cathode, giving sodium hydroxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The object to be plated is the cathode, where metal ions are reduced onto it.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In a cell the cathode is the positive electrode: reduction happens there and electrons arrive from the external circuit.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The standard cell EMF is the difference between the standard reduction potentials of the cathode and the anode.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    0.34 − (−0.76) = 1.10 V.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zinc is oxidised and gives up electrons, which travel from the zinc anode to the copper cathode.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ions move through the bridge to balance the charge produced by the electrode reactions.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Iron is oxidised to hydrated iron(III) oxide, and both water and oxygen are needed.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zinc is more reactive than iron, so it corrodes in preference and protects the iron.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zinc has a more negative reduction potential, so it is oxidised first.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Moist air is precisely the condition that causes rusting.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Tin goes from +2 to +4, so it is oxidised and reduces iron from +3 to +2.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each hydrogen is +1, so each oxygen must be −1 for the molecule to be neutral.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2x + 3(−2) = −2, so 2x = +4 and x = +2.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen ions are reduced at the cathode and water is oxidised to oxygen at the anode.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    No oxidation number changes in an acid–base neutralisation.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The more negative electrode is oxidised, so it is the anode.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Metals above hydrogen displace it from dilute acids, giving hydrogen gas.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Copper's reduction potential is positive, so it cannot reduce hydrogen ions.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Copper atoms at the anode are oxidised and pass into solution, so the anode loses mass as the cathode gains it.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Copper is below hydrogen in the series, so copper ions are preferentially reduced.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1 + x + 3(−2) = 0, so x = +5.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Chlorine goes from 0 to −1 in NaCl and to +1 in NaClO, so it is disproportionated.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The more positive the reduction potential, the stronger the oxidising agent.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A positive cell EMF means the cell reaction is feasible and the cell can do electrical work.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Standard conditions are 1 atm, 1 M H⁺ and 298 K, and the potential is defined as zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen ions gain electrons, so they are reduced and act as the oxidising agent.
  ]

